{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1921 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "67d5a910c3038639dcb35f11936c4ffb69d656fcce7f74bbc32b0e6888c3d530" :edb/db-before-t 1920 :edb/db-after-t 1921 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535233 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:07:51.137Z" 13194139535233 true] [17592186064109 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338977 13194139535233 true] [17592186064165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338967 13194139535233 true] [17592186064185 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338970 13194139535233 true] [17592186068751 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338951 13194139535233 true] [17592186078476 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338958 13194139535233 true] [17592186100802 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338961 13194139535233 true] [17592186171888 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338944 13194139535233 true] [17592186307348 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/0cec723bf4e6886ca0715e9e02c658a7ee0e1f24555ea3946f95aa2b12b863be.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139535233 false] [17592186307348 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059760/assets/0cec723bf4e6886ca0715e9e02c658a7ee0e1f24555ea3946f95aa2b12b863be.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139535233 true] [17592186307348 1145 "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,2)$, $(-2,2),$ $(2,-2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/c688b1ee540e732329e597fb97a0595f619dbc4ff0aad86b6a01f1bdb51ae66b.png)" 13194139535233 false] [17592186307348 1145 "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,2)$, $(-2,2),$ $(2,-2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059760/assets/c688b1ee540e732329e597fb97a0595f619dbc4ff0aad86b6a01f1bdb51ae66b.png)" 13194139535233 true] [17592186308833 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/c010f583fc33ee3c5335c16c0aaf1c20c2e5cf82d49fe7cf14e8e5661bf202fc.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" 13194139535233 false] [17592186308833 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059760/assets/c010f583fc33ee3c5335c16c0aaf1c20c2e5cf82d49fe7cf14e8e5661bf202fc.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338944 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338945 13194139535233 true] [17592186338944 1089 "q-113607" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338944 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535233 true] [17592186338944 1100 "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})+[]\n$$" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338944 1113 #uuid "31c261c0-c13d-56df-97d3-69bf96671591" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338944 1145 "To find $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $6$ and position $3$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3}) \\\\  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{3}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{4}) \\\\  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5}) \\\\  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{6}) \\\\  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3}).$" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338946 13194139535233 true] [17592186338945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338947 13194139535233 true] [17592186338945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338948 13194139535233 true] [17592186338945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338949 13194139535233 true] [17592186338945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338950 13194139535233 true] [17592186338945 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535233 true] [17592186338945 1041 "selection" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338945 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338950 13194139535233 true] [17592186338945 1110 #uuid "7a65eb51-3516-5f48-b5a6-84c50f962faf" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338946 1034 "(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2})" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338946 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535233 true] [17592186338946 1111 #uuid "c9627a6e-c790-5025-b101-12ea1591e8fd" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338947 1034 "(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338947 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535233 true] [17592186338947 1111 #uuid "74876d33-40ef-5630-9a88-6a3381c99893" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338948 1034 "(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2})" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338948 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535233 true] [17592186338948 1111 #uuid "e89eeffb-1442-5ad9-8c7e-466d35845290" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338949 1034 "(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1})" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338949 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535233 true] [17592186338949 1111 #uuid "131f526d-0f02-57ba-9efd-0f15ea3a3905" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338950 1034 "(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3})" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338950 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535233 true] [17592186338950 1111 #uuid "d94e3891-28c7-5c24-b058-d0e91d5957a9" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338951 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338952 13194139535233 true] [17592186338951 1089 "q-115274" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338951 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535233 true] [17592186338951 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059760/assets/402ae2f9f3d1e0ce970a18b68332400d261962e40ebde9acdc179727e94f32ac.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality  $(x+1)(x-4)>0.$" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338951 1113 #uuid "73608df6-a2a9-5efe-b3f4-d968a8f235fb" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338951 1145 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-4)>0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=(x+1)(x-4)\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059760/assets/98537a1ab5afd590c47a2605d6d0bc8858f51e838033cc61a38845b3e8c87a53.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-1$ or $x>4.$" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338953 13194139535233 true] [17592186338952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338954 13194139535233 true] [17592186338952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338955 13194139535233 true] [17592186338952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338956 13194139535233 true] [17592186338952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338957 13194139535233 true] [17592186338952 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535233 true] [17592186338952 1041 "selection" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338952 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338953 13194139535233 true] [17592186338952 1110 #uuid "d8ff6265-a9a6-5700-a27d-6a1a7e781291" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338953 1034 "$x<-1$ or $x>4$" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338953 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535233 true] [17592186338953 1111 #uuid "e7c390cf-4cd1-50f5-800b-879fea95f549" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338954 1034 "-4<x<-1" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338954 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535233 true] [17592186338954 1111 #uuid "8c61ba4a-50b5-5548-a6bb-235cc6daf555" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338955 1034 "-1<x<4" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338955 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535233 true] [17592186338955 1111 #uuid "e1f7479f-7f79-5184-bc71-130f64f3e07b" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338956 1034 "$x<-4$ or $x>1$" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338956 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535233 true] [17592186338956 1111 #uuid "45e14bd3-d5b4-5f08-87a1-f70494cd1f3b" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338957 1034 "x>0" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338957 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535233 true] [17592186338957 1111 #uuid "d7374576-071a-5fa1-8521-87a67d55ea2f" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338958 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338959 13194139535233 true] [17592186338958 1089 "q-302331" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338958 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535233 true] [17592186338958 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{3}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338958 1113 #uuid "56ee2d50-7525-539b-8670-a399c4adf79c" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338958 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=3$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{3}} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-9)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3})}{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{3})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3})}{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3})}{x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-3)}(x+3)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3})}{\\cancel{x-3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(x+3)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =(3+3)(\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =6(2\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338960 13194139535233 true] [17592186338959 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535233 true] [17592186338959 1041 "field-1" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338959 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338960 13194139535233 true] [17592186338959 1110 #uuid "dfe93357-b04f-50e0-bc40-5cc2c2c4bcb4" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338960 1034 "12\\sqrt{3}" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338960 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535233 true] [17592186338960 1111 #uuid "41956e2e-8d3a-56ed-b519-8d3967b79417" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338961 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338962 13194139535233 true] [17592186338961 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338965 13194139535233 true] [17592186338961 1089 "q-321410" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338961 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535233 true] [17592186338961 1100 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-3t+2,\\,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{t},\\,t\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535233 true] [17592186338961 1113 #uuid "032b7386-bebf-584d-aa51-c55bac49aec8" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338961 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-3t+2\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{2-x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{2-x}{3}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2-x}{3})} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{3}{x-2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 0$ and $x=-3t+2$ , we must have $x\\ne 2.$ \n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{x-2},\\,x\\ne 2.\n$$" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338962 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338963 13194139535233 true] [17592186338962 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338964 13194139535233 true] [17592186338962 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535233 true] [17592186338962 1041 "field-1" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338962 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338963 13194139535233 true] [17592186338962 1110 #uuid "745fea25-6d62-5921-9c24-a67f3114aeab" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338963 1034 "\\frac{3}{x-2}" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338963 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535233 true] [17592186338963 1111 #uuid "c8840ae0-7cbf-521d-b355-e1b29f10030a" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338964 1034 "\\frac{3}{x+2}" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338964 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535233 true] [17592186338964 1111 #uuid "03109a29-2a80-586b-b64f-c5695518e8a3" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338965 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338966 13194139535233 true] [17592186338965 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535233 true] [17592186338965 1041 "field-2" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338965 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338966 13194139535233 true] [17592186338965 1110 #uuid "c98a97b5-6183-5310-9855-2bb3c4c205aa" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338966 1034 "2" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338966 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535233 true] [17592186338966 1111 #uuid "25a7fd8d-e163-50ff-851f-0ac51b5dbaee" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338967 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338968 13194139535233 true] [17592186338967 1089 "q-342591" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338967 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535233 true] [17592186338967 1100 "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-2)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $-2$ | $-3$ |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $7$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(-2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338967 1113 #uuid "97e97885-5364-548d-a866-b48f29ce6c12" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338967 1145 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(-2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(-2)=5$ and ${f}^{′}(5)=-3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338969 13194139535233 true] [17592186338968 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535233 true] [17592186338968 1041 "field-1" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338968 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338969 13194139535233 true] [17592186338968 1110 #uuid "b80233ac-f41c-57d6-b390-29647f4da7e8" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338969 1034 "-\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338969 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535233 true] [17592186338969 1111 #uuid "08d80e3c-2f4b-54fa-bc59-8271c54ea8b8" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338970 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338971 13194139535233 true] [17592186338970 1089 "q-47911" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338970 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535233 true] [17592186338970 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059760/assets/bb8959927fc561f9fc6e021b690c43f4ab57b169aa1526390e02c8d48386bfe3.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the critical points?" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338970 1113 #uuid "cfc1f38a-b4e3-5848-bc27-d254a3cdbd67" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338970 1145 "The critical points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ doesn't exist. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal or where the tangent does not exist, respectively. \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that \n\n- at $x=2$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(0)=0,$ while\n\n- at $x=4$ there is a sharp corner, so ${f}^{′}(4)$ doesn't exist\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059760/assets/60f26a8aa758ae20b2b56325d822379d407276ffd6987f56a1e7934a6406c4d2.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, there are two critical points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=2$ and $x=4.$" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338971 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338972 13194139535233 true] [17592186338971 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338973 13194139535233 true] [17592186338971 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338974 13194139535233 true] [17592186338971 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338975 13194139535233 true] [17592186338971 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338976 13194139535233 true] [17592186338971 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535233 true] [17592186338971 1041 "selection" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338971 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338976 13194139535233 true] [17592186338971 1110 #uuid "594f89de-b0ea-5b81-84f5-a93099ab18f0" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338972 1034 "$x=3$ and $x=5$" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338972 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535233 true] [17592186338972 1111 #uuid "0033bc47-e537-5394-8f40-841d466de5d7" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338973 1034 "$x=2$ and $x=6$" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338973 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535233 true] [17592186338973 1111 #uuid "2ecbea32-ea9d-5a56-b13f-bde8e585ba41" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338974 1034 "$x=1$ and $x=4$" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338974 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535233 true] [17592186338974 1111 #uuid "1e18b132-a788-5718-a65a-02fd187ee966" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338975 1034 "$x=4$ only" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338975 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535233 true] [17592186338975 1111 #uuid "84767ecd-933a-5540-b2f5-a5e0d11c176f" 13194139535233 true] [17592186338976 1034 "$x=2$ an